  {
    "episode_id": 90416,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90416.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 71,
    "turn_text": "It probably changed us in a number of different ways of things. But I will say that, you know, so, you know, we grew up very middle class. I mean, we were growing up. We didn't have a lot of money. I remember at one point that this washer didn't work for years. And we were the dishwashers, the kids. And we had a chair to prop the oven door closed because it wouldn't stay closed by itself until mom and dad could afford to get it fixed. But as the company grew, when I joined, we had about 100 people working at Ameritrade. And when I left, we had like 6,000 people. So you know, but that was over a period of about almost a dozen years. So it did grow rapidly for a company. But one of the things my dad always made us as people working at the company, he didn't want to sell any stocks. So we really couldn't monetize, even though the stock increased greatly in value, we couldn't really monetize it. Now our salaries went up so, and then we got paid nicely. But it wasn't like it became like this super wealthy thing all at once. It's really been over a period of time. And Ameritrade has continued to be successful, and the stock prices has gone up. Yeah, yeah.",
