  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90416,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90416.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 103,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'd say a couple things. And I think, well, first of all, my daughter, Margot, who was seven at the time, came up to me and said, Daddy, I know you're very sad that you lost the election, but we're glad to have you home. How are you going to feel bad about that? The other thing I learned is that the sun came up the next morning. My wife was still my wife. My kids were still my kids. My friends were still my friends. The world didn't end. It was okay. Here's the thing. If you don't take risks, you will not make progress. Sometimes when you take risks, things don't turn out the way you think. That's the definition of a risk. Sometimes you've got to fail and you learn way more from your failures than you do your successes. And I got to tell you, I love the experience. Even though I've lost and got my tail, I had to wait. I absolutely loved it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "My daughter Margot was seven years old at the time.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Margot told me she knew I was sad about losing the election but that we were glad to have me home.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The sun rose the following morning.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Margot asked how I could feel bad after her statement.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Taking risks is necessary for making progress.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "My wife remained my wife after the loss.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "My children remained my children after the loss.",
        "confidence": 0.92
