  {
    "episode_id": 90416,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90416.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 158,
    "turn_text": "Well, one of the things I did run on was running government more like a business. And I'm kind of getting your question here. Republicans and Democrats will argue over the size and scope of government. If you want to really boil it down, that's kind of what we're asking. What should government do? But there are things we agree it should do. And those things we should do really, really well, right? We should be operationally excellent. We should be really doing a great job of providing services. We should reply the same lessons that businesses use every day to do a great job of providing their services to government, to make government run really well. And so that's one of the things that I campaigned on and that's one of the things we've done as I've been governor is really improve the level of service we're providing for people. You know, so what to me it's about like I'm obviously as a Republican I'm more on the side of hey we should be looking at what are the government's core functions and not getting in the way of the private sector as they're trying to do things and that sort of thing. And we've done a great job, frankly, of improving level services. I'll just share with you one example. We have an economic assistance line. It's where people apply for food assistance or energy or whatever, but the SNAP program, the supplemental nutritional assistance program, is one of the ways you can apply. Otherwise, no food stamps. In August of 2014, before I got elected, people were waiting on average 23 minutes to get their phone calls answered. That means some people wait a lot longer. What does that say to our customers about how",
